<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Egbert of Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/839">839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Ecgberht of Wessex, Ecgberht, King of Wessex</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Egbert was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from 802 to 839. After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beornwulf-of-mercia">Beornwulf of Mercia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Battle of Ellendun</a> in 825, he took Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex, and in 829 he briefly conquered Mercia itself and received the submission of Northumbria, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> calls him a bretwalda. His victories made Wessex the leading English kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex.jpg" alt="Egbert of Wessex" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egbert_-_MS_Royal_14_B_V.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Historians do not agree on Ecgberht's ancestry. His father was Ealhmund, thought to be the king of Kent who appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> in 784. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a> dominated Kent in the late 780s, and Ecgberht was forced into exile at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> by Offa and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beorhtric-of-wessex">Beorhtric of Wessex</a>. The Chronicle says he spent three years there, though many modern accounts assume a scribal error for thirteen. When Beorhtric died in 802, Ecgberht came to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, probably with the support of Charlemagne and perhaps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Little is known of the first twenty years of the reign, but there is no evidence that Ecgberht submitted to the Mercian king Cenwulf, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> seems to have kept its independence. In 825 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beornwulf-of-mercia">Beornwulf of Mercia</a> at Ellandun, now Wroughton near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swindon">Swindon</a>, a battle that ended Mercian domination of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. His son Æthelwulf went to Kent with a troop, and the men of Kent, Essex, Surrey and Sussex submitted. When East Anglia asked for his protection, Beornwulf and his successor Ludeca were killed invading it.</p><p>In 829 Ecgberht invaded Mercia and drove King Wiglaf into exile, and he issued coins as king of Mercia. The Chronicle says that he conquered Mercia and all that was south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>, and that he was the eighth king to be bretwalda, or wide-ruler. Later that year he received the submission of the Northumbrian king, probably Eanred, at Dore, though one historian suggests the meeting was more likely a mutual recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. In 830 he led an expedition against the Welsh, the high point of his influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 830 Wiglaf regained the Mercian throne, probably by a rebellion against West Saxon rule, and Ecgberht's wider dominance ended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> kept Kent, Sussex and Surrey, ruled by Æthelwulf as a subking, and the independent existence of the kingdoms of Kent and Sussex was over. In the south-west Ecgberht was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> at Carhampton in 836 but won a battle against them and the West Welsh at Hingston Down in 838.</p><p>He died in 839 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, as were his son Æthelwulf, his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> and his great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>. His victories fundamentally changed the politics of Anglo-Saxon England, and his descendants ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and later all England continuously until 1013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ecgberht, King of Wessex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army was broken by <b>Ecgberht of Wessex</b> at the Battle of Ellendun in 825 and could not withstand the Great Heathen Army, which occupied Repton in 873–874.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ecgberht</b>, brother of King Eadberht, secured the archiepiscopal pallium in 735, and for two decades the brothers ruled kingdom and church together.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 825 <b>Ecgberht</b> defeated Beornwulf of Mercia at the Battle of Ellendun, near Swindon, a victory that brought Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex under West Saxon rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After 825 Essex submitted to <b>Ecgberht</b> of the Kingdom of Wessex, and later fell within the Danelaw.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-essex">Kingdom of Essex</a></li><li><blockquote>In 825 <b>Egbert of Wessex</b> took Kent, which was ruled as a sub-kingdom by West Saxon princes until it was merged into Wessex in the 860s, later becoming an English shire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kent">Kingdom of Kent</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Ecgberht</b>'s victory at Ellendun in 825, Sussex submitted to the Kingdom of Wessex and became a shire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sussex">Kingdom of Sussex</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
